  "response": "Here's a Python function named `format_currency(n)` that formats a number as a string with a `$` in front and commas every 3 digits. For negative numbers, it places a minus sign before the dollar sign:\n\n```python\ndef format_currency(n):\n    is_negative = n < 0\n    n_str = str(abs(n))\n    \n    parts = []\n    for i in range(0, len(n_str), 3):\n        parts.append(n_str[i:i + 3])\n    formatted = ','.join(reversed(parts))\n    \n    sign = \"-\" if is_negative else \"\"\n    return f\"{sign}${formatted}\"\n```\n\n### Example Usage:\n\n```python\nprint(format_currency(4640039))      # $4,640,039\nprint(format_currency(-1234567))     # -$1,234,567\nprint(format_currency(0))            # $0\nprint(format_currency(1000))         # $1,000\nprint(format_currency(-10))          # -$10\n```\n\n### Alternative (Using Built-in Formatting):\n\nPython's built-in f-string formatting with `:,.2f` or `:,f` can also format numbers with thousand separators. However, it doesn't include a `$` sign by default and adds decimal points unless disabled. Therefore, this custom function provides more precise control over formatting as required.",
